<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kirti Sri Rajasinha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned <a href="/years/1747">1747</a>–<a href="/years/1782">1782</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kirti Sri Rajasinha was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> from 1747 to 1782, of the Nayak dynasty of south Indian origin. A patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, he invited monks from Siam in 1753 to restore the higher ordination, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyam-nikaya">Siyam Nikaya</a>, and restored many temples. He fought a war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, who took Kandy in 1765 and imposed a treaty in 1766.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kirti-sri-rajasinha.jpg" alt="Kirti Sri Rajasinha" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:118_King_Rajasingha_makes_Ven_Saranankara_Sangharaja_(19820173144).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born in 1734, Kirti Sri Rajasinha was a prince of the Madurai Nayak Dynasty and the brother-in-law of Sri Vijaya Rajasinha, whom he succeeded as the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>. As a Tamil-speaking prince he at first found it hard to win the trust of a largely Sinhalese Buddhist people, but gained acceptance through his devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. He married a daughter of Nadukattu Sami Nayakkar in 1749 and later further Nayakkar queens from Madurai, with whom he had no children; by a Sinhalese consort, a daughter of the late Dissava of Bintenna, he had six daughters and two sons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Guided by the monk Weliwita Sri Saranankara Thero, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> help, he brought monks from Siam to restore the higher ordination. The Siamese monk Upali Thera arrived in 1753, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyam-nikaya">Siyam Nikaya</a> was formed under the king's patronage. He built the inner temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relic-of-the-tooth-of-the-buddha">Sacred Tooth Relic</a> and the Gangarama Raja Maha Vihara in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, rebuilt the Munneswaram temple near Chilaw, and had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a> chronicle continued from the time of Parakramabahu IV of Dambadeniya. He encouraged translations of Buddhist texts from Pali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> into Sinhala.</p><p>In 1761 his forces attacked and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> forts at Matara, Katuwana, Tangalle, Marakade and Urubokke. Seeking allies, he asked the Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-st-george">Fort St George</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> for help, and in May 1762 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s John Pybus landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trincomalee">Trincomalee</a> and met him, though their talks reached no agreement. Through 1763 the king sought peace but refused the Dutch demands for territory and the whole coastline. The Dutch attacked again in 1764 and 1765, with heavy losses on both sides, and the king withdrew to Hanguranketha. Pressed by troubles within the kingdom, he signed the treaty of 1766, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> lost its access to the sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 2 January 1782. The claim of Mampitiya's sons was passed over, and the throne went to the king's brother, Rajadhi Rajasinha, who continued his policies until the British conquest of 1815. Life-sized portraits of the king stand in the temples he restored, and wooden statues of him are kept at Dambulla and at the Malwatta temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kirti Sri Rajasinha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1739 the Nayak kings of south Indian origin ruled, and <b>Kirti Sri Rajasinha</b> revived the Buddhist monastic order with monks from Siam.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kandy">Kingdom of Kandy</a></li></ul></section>
